# Verified against the running device with `ls -Z`: every file directly under
# soc:lenovo_kb/ carries sysfs_kb, except wakeup/, which the kernel's own
# wakeup-source labelling claims as sysfs_wakeup. The order matters — the more
# specific wakeup entry has to come after the directory entry.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/soc:lenovo_kb            u:object_r:sysfs_kb:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/soc:lenovo_kb/wakeup      u:object_r:sysfs_wakeup:s0

# The stylus and folio knobs. Stock leaves these as plain "proc" and lets its
# touchscreen HAL write any proc file; labelling them lets that HAL be granted
# these four alone (see hal_lenovo.te).
genfscon proc /pen_type                                        u:object_r:proc_lenovo_pen:s0
genfscon proc /pen_attached                                    u:object_r:proc_lenovo_pen:s0
genfscon proc /pen_wakeup_mode                                 u:object_r:proc_lenovo_pen:s0
genfscon proc /folio_case_mode                                 u:object_r:proc_lenovo_pen:s0

# The other /proc nodes the touchscreen HAL opens. See file.te.
#
# ⚠️ The authority for this list is the HAL BINARY, not the stock .rc:
#
#   strings vendor/bin/hw/vendor.lenovo.hardware.touchscreen-service \
#     | grep '^/proc/'          -> 13 paths
#
# The stock .rc is written for a whole SKU line and chowns paths that do not
# exist on this unit. Taking the list from it put /proc/gesture_control here --
# a node that does not exist (verified on the device: `ls -Z` says MISSING) --
# while leaving /proc/gesture_mode and /proc/support_pen, which the HAL really
# does open, at plain u:object_r:proc:s0 and therefore denied under enforcing.
#
# file.te already recorded that gesture_control "is not created on this unit";
# what was missed is that the real node is gesture_mode, under a different name.
#
# gesture_mode and support_pen are root:root 0666 on stock rather than
# system:system like the other eleven, because the stock .rc never chowns them.
# Mode 0666 means DAC is not the gate here -- SELinux is.
genfscon proc /panel_direction                                 u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /game_mode                                       u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /game_edge                                       u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /edge_grid_zone                                  u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /report_threshold                                u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /HighReportRate                                  u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /haptics                                         u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /gesture_mode                                    u:object_r:proc_lenovo_touch:s0
genfscon proc /support_pen                                     u:object_r:proc_lenovo_pen:s0

# /proc/nvt_fw_version is deliberately NOT labelled here, even though the stock
# vendor_sepolicy.cil labels it (vendor_proc_tpver) and grants platform_app and
# system_app read access. Recording the decision because a diff against stock
# will keep surfacing it as "the one device-specific label we are missing".
#
# By this file's own rule -- the authority is the consumer, not the stock policy
# -- it has no consumer here. A binary-safe search finds zero references to
# nvt_fw_version in anything this tree installs, and, notably, zero in the
# factory's vendor and system *binaries* too. Its reader is a Lenovo
# factory-test app, i.e. dex on the ZUI product partition, which PixelOS
# replaces wholesale.
#
# Adding it would also mean vendor policy granting two coredomains
# (platform_app, system_app) access to a vendor-labelled type, which is the
# direction Treble's neverallows exist to discourage. If a consumer ever ships,
# add the label and the grant together with it.

# Awinic smart-amplifier nodes (aw882xx_chipid and the driver's other per-device
# attributes). Four AW88461 on QUP i2c bus 5 at 0x34, 0x35, 0x36 and 0x37.
# Path read off the running device rather than guessed:
#   /sys/devices/platform/soc/ac0000.qcom,qupv3_1_geni_se/a94000.i2c/i2c-5/5-0034/aw882xx_chipid
# The four clients are named one by one rather than labelling the bus: i2c-5
# also carries 5-003e (AW37504 display bias) and 5-0064 (RTC6226 FM tuner), and
# genfscon prefix-matches, so a bus-level entry would hand the audio HAL read
# access to two unrelated devices.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/ac0000.qcom,qupv3_1_geni_se/a94000.i2c/i2c-5/5-0034 u:object_r:sysfs_awinic:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/ac0000.qcom,qupv3_1_geni_se/a94000.i2c/i2c-5/5-0035 u:object_r:sysfs_awinic:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/ac0000.qcom,qupv3_1_geni_se/a94000.i2c/i2c-5/5-0036 u:object_r:sysfs_awinic:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/ac0000.qcom,qupv3_1_geni_se/a94000.i2c/i2c-5/5-0037 u:object_r:sysfs_awinic:s0

# Lenovo/Tinno per-component hardware inventory. hal_sensors_default writes its
# own entry here at probe; see sepolicy/vendor/measured.te for the readout and
# for why this gets a type instead of the sysfs blanket stock uses.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/product-device-info                          u:object_r:sysfs_product_device_info:s0
